WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.610 --> 00:00:08.260
When teenage nerd Peter Parker is bitten by
a radioactive spider, he gains a weird selection

00:00:08.260 --> 00:00:12.700
of arachnid powers and spends the rest of
his days balancing vigilante crimefighting

00:00:12.700 --> 00:00:14.879
against leading a normal life.

00:00:14.879 --> 00:00:18.740
Except it's never really been that simple
for our friendly, neighborhood Spider-Man,

00:00:18.740 --> 00:00:23.340
and his history has so many twists and turns
that it's not easy to keep track of his tangled

00:00:23.340 --> 00:00:24.340
web.

00:00:24.340 --> 00:00:25.750
A true original?

00:00:25.750 --> 00:00:32.460
In 1954, Halloween costume manufacturer Ben
Cooper Inc. began selling a "Spider Man" outfit.

00:00:32.460 --> 00:00:37.000
The kid-sized duds were little more than a
yellow ensemble covered with drawings of webs,

00:00:37.000 --> 00:00:41.280
and the words "Spider Man" written across
the forehead, giving the overall impression

00:00:41.280 --> 00:00:46.150
of, well, a poorly-dressed idiot who'd recently
escaped from a basement or something.

00:00:46.150 --> 00:00:52.890
Years later, Marvel's Spider-Man made his
debut in 1962's Amazing Fantasy #15, the final

00:00:52.890 --> 00:00:55.420
issue of a doomed anthology series.

00:00:55.420 --> 00:01:00.280
While Cooper's earlier Spider Man may seem
like a huge coincidence, the case is complicated

00:01:00.280 --> 00:01:05.720
by rumors that Jack Kirby, the original artist
for Spider-Man, before Steve Ditko took over,

00:01:05.720 --> 00:01:08.390
was working for Ben Cooper at the time.

00:01:08.390 --> 00:01:13.650
Comics site Newsarama explored the case and
got a terse reply from Ditko...and silence

00:01:13.650 --> 00:01:17.830
from Marvel, which definitely sets our spider-senses
tingling.

00:01:17.830 --> 00:01:19.900
Spider origins redux

00:01:19.900 --> 00:01:24.360
Artist Jack Kirby had his hands in the creation
of just about every major comic character

00:01:24.360 --> 00:01:25.490
you've ever heard of.

00:01:25.490 --> 00:01:30.560
While the first appearance of Spider-Man was
written by Stan Lee and drawn by Steve Ditko,

00:01:30.560 --> 00:01:34.910
Lee originally had Kirby draw the first five
pages of Spidey's debut.

00:01:34.910 --> 00:01:37.090
The pages didn't hit the mark and were scrapped.

00:01:37.090 --> 00:01:42.270
For the final version, Lee's original plot
and Kirby's costume designs were changed,

00:01:42.270 --> 00:01:46.490
and Kirby ultimately only drew the cover of
Amazing Fantasy #15.

00:01:46.490 --> 00:01:50.950
Unfortunately, this didn't stop Kirby's heirs
from trying to claim payment for Marvel's

00:01:50.950 --> 00:01:53.860
use of Spider-Man in 2009.

00:01:53.860 --> 00:01:59.120
Even Lee, Kirby's everlasting partner in mighty
Marvel creativity, denied the Kirby family's

00:01:59.120 --> 00:02:00.140
claim.

00:02:00.140 --> 00:02:04.990
According to Deadline, the suit was settled
on undisclosed terms in 2014, which is even

00:02:04.990 --> 00:02:08.240
more suspicious than Norman Osborn's crazy
cornrows.

00:02:08.240 --> 00:02:09.689
20 Ingram Street

00:02:09.689 --> 00:02:17.110
Over the course of 1989's Amazing Spider-Man
#316 and 317, readers were shown two halves

00:02:17.110 --> 00:02:21.740
of a change-of-address form filled out by
Peter Parker, and only curious readers who

00:02:21.740 --> 00:02:26.950
assembled them saw that the Parkers were now
living at 20 Ingram Street in Forest Hills,

00:02:26.950 --> 00:02:27.950
NY.

00:02:27.950 --> 00:02:31.920
Whether or not this was an Easter egg planted
by writer David Michelinie or artist Todd

00:02:31.920 --> 00:02:38.800
McFarlane is unknown, but as of 1974, there
really was a family named Parker at that address,

00:02:38.800 --> 00:02:42.500
who soon began receiving mail addressed to
the fictional Peter.

00:02:42.500 --> 00:02:47.280
When the New York Times explained the story
in 2002, they also discovered that 19 Ingram

00:02:47.280 --> 00:02:53.040
Street was home to a family named Osborne—just
one letter off from Spider-Man's arch-nemesis,

00:02:53.040 --> 00:02:54.040
Norman Osborn.

00:02:54.040 --> 00:02:55.040
Back in black

00:02:55.040 --> 00:02:59.070
When he's not wearing his red-and-blue duds,
Spider-Man is known for wearing a stealthy

00:02:59.070 --> 00:03:04.170
and slimming black, which he found in an alien
vending machine during 1984's Secret Wars

00:03:04.170 --> 00:03:07.000
mini-series and decided to slap on his body.

00:03:07.000 --> 00:03:11.400
What readers might not know is that the black
costume wasn't an original idea from Marvel,

00:03:11.400 --> 00:03:15.000
but one sent in by a fan for a contest in
1982.

00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:19.560
While Randy Schueller's costume idea apparently
involved underarm webbing, which didn't make

00:03:19.560 --> 00:03:24.819
it into this sketch, and a red logo, the similarities
to the official Black suit remain.

00:03:24.819 --> 00:03:30.150
Schueller was paid for his idea: $220 dollars,
which would be about $550 today.

00:03:30.150 --> 00:03:35.700
Schueller's other ideas would later be incorporated
into additional Spidey costume ideas: unstable

00:03:35.700 --> 00:03:40.690
molecules and thought control were both used
by Spidey's Future Foundation and Iron Spider

00:03:40.690 --> 00:03:45.480
costumes respectively, but Schueller was never
given any actual credit, according to his

00:03:45.480 --> 00:03:48.220
own post on Comic Book Resources.

00:03:48.220 --> 00:03:49.220
Center of the web

00:03:49.220 --> 00:03:53.580
Comics fans might not think about it much,
but Spider-Man is the absolute center of the

00:03:53.580 --> 00:03:55.160
Marvel multiverse.

00:03:55.160 --> 00:03:59.470
Even though heroes like Captain America and
Iron Man get top billing, there are more versions

00:03:59.470 --> 00:04:03.909
of Spider-Man across the multiverse than any
other hero, and any time we visit another

00:04:03.909 --> 00:04:08.840
reality, Spider-Man is so iconic and consistent
that he's always there, and generally used

00:04:08.840 --> 00:04:12.470
as a compass to differentiate the different
planes of existence.

00:04:12.470 --> 00:04:17.320
While the main Marvel continuity, Earth-616,
has its own fair share of Spideys, there are

00:04:17.320 --> 00:04:22.979
literally hundreds of alternates, including
Spider-Ham of Earth-8311, the flaming skull-headed

00:04:22.979 --> 00:04:28.219
Ghost Spider of Earth-11638… and a whole
lot of dead Spider-Men, since Peter seems

00:04:28.219 --> 00:04:31.469
to get caught in the ol' bug zapper of life
pretty often.

00:04:31.469 --> 00:04:33.259
The cruelest revenge

00:04:33.259 --> 00:04:38.159
Spider-Man has probably had the roughest life
of any superhero; he doesn't have Batman's

00:04:38.159 --> 00:04:42.689
wealth, and he doesn't have Superman's strength,
but he accomplishes pretty much the same heroic

00:04:42.689 --> 00:04:43.689
stuff.

00:04:43.689 --> 00:04:47.580
But the very worst thing that ever happened
to Spider-Man was being cheated on by his

00:04:47.580 --> 00:04:51.369
girlfriend, Gwen Stacy, with his arch-nemesis,
Norman Osborn, a.k.a.

00:04:51.369 --> 00:04:52.800
the Green Goblin.

00:04:52.800 --> 00:04:58.169
While this was never spoken of during Gwen's
life, the 1999 storyline "Sins Past" revealed

00:04:58.169 --> 00:05:03.169
the ultimate betrayal: Gwen had kept her terrible
goblin babies secret, and because of their

00:05:03.169 --> 00:05:08.259
unique DNA, they aged rapidly and came after
Peter Parker, because that's what goblins

00:05:08.259 --> 00:05:09.259
do.

00:05:09.259 --> 00:05:13.899
Everyone—Peter Parker and comic readers
included—hated the whole thing.

00:05:13.899 --> 00:05:14.899
Spider-Magic

00:05:14.899 --> 00:05:18.719
Everyone knows that Spider-Man's powers are
purely scientific in nature, because being

00:05:18.719 --> 00:05:23.169
bit by a radioactive animal automatically
transfers its powers to you.

00:05:23.169 --> 00:05:24.699
That's just basic biology.

00:05:24.699 --> 00:05:30.009
Except in 2001, the arrival of a character
named Ezekiel revealed that Spidey's superhuman

00:05:30.009 --> 00:05:34.711
abilities were actually kinda magic, and that
fateful radioactive spider bit Peter Parker

00:05:34.711 --> 00:05:37.909
on purpose, because of cosmic mystical machinations.

00:05:37.909 --> 00:05:42.699
It's a bit like the midichlorians of the Marvel
Universe, but it also kinda explains why most

00:05:42.699 --> 00:05:45.349
of Spidey's bad guys are animal-themed.

00:05:45.349 --> 00:05:46.589
Deal with the Devil

00:05:46.589 --> 00:05:51.719
The apex of weird Spider-Man storylines is
Peter Parker's deal with the devil, Mephisto.

00:05:51.719 --> 00:05:56.529
In exchange for the survival of his dear,
decrepit, nearly-deceased-anyway Aunt May,

00:05:56.529 --> 00:06:00.449
Spidey would give up his marriage and future
kids with Mary Jane, forcing him straight

00:06:00.449 --> 00:06:02.169
back into the friend zone.

00:06:02.169 --> 00:06:03.919
Spidey's reason for taking this deal?

00:06:03.919 --> 00:06:07.990
Peter's Aunt May was hovering between life
and death because she'd been put in danger

00:06:07.990 --> 00:06:12.849
after he revealed his secret identity during
Civil War, and he'd come to the sensible conclusion

00:06:12.849 --> 00:06:16.649
that taking off his mask in front of the world
had been a big mistake.

00:06:16.649 --> 00:06:21.039
In return for Peter sacrificing his life with
Mary Jane, Mephisto made the world forget

00:06:21.039 --> 00:06:25.939
he was Spider-Man, thus protecting his loved
ones from the line of supervillain fire.

00:06:25.939 --> 00:06:27.460
Crossover madness

00:06:27.460 --> 00:06:30.970
Spidey is a guy who gets around in some pretty
weird places.

00:06:30.970 --> 00:06:36.559
In 1978, Peter and Mary Jane attend a taping
of Saturday Night Live, and a mix-up between

00:06:36.559 --> 00:06:41.960
villain Silver Samurai and John Belushi's
samurai character causes an issue-long ruckus

00:06:41.960 --> 00:06:45.610
featuring the entire 1978 SNL cast.

00:06:45.610 --> 00:06:51.270
In 1993, Marvel had the rights to publish
a comic series based on Ren &amp; Stimpy, so Spider-Man

00:06:51.270 --> 00:06:56.080
ended up battling Powdered Toast Man in those
pages—and getting one-punched through a

00:06:56.080 --> 00:06:57.999
wall by the bread-headed hero.

00:06:57.999 --> 00:07:02.889
And in 2009, Spider-Man rescues none other
than Barack Obama from the Chameleon, who

00:07:02.889 --> 00:07:06.530
decides to impersonate the president-elect
moments before the inauguration.

00:07:06.530 --> 00:07:08.990
And that's truly only the tip of the weird-berg.

00:07:08.990 --> 00:07:11.589
So many spiders

00:07:11.589 --> 00:07:16.240
At one point in Marvel's history, Spider-Man
was so popular that he starred in four books

00:07:16.240 --> 00:07:21.660
at once, making it nearly impossible to keep
track of his adventures, or even take a nap.

00:07:21.660 --> 00:07:26.419
All of it came to a head when Norman Osborn
framed Spider-Man for murder, forcing Parker

00:07:26.419 --> 00:07:31.380
to drop his heroic persona and take on a different
heroic mantle…or four.

00:07:31.380 --> 00:07:36.249
As Hornet and Prodigy, Peter continued his
heroics, and as Dusk and Ricochet, he was

00:07:36.249 --> 00:07:40.889
able to infiltrate New York's criminal underworld
to find the real murderer.

00:07:40.889 --> 00:07:45.080
Spidey was able to drop his alternate identities,
but they were later taken up by a group called

00:07:45.080 --> 00:07:49.550
the Slingers, who were quickly beaten by the
New Warriors in a game of basketball and pretty

00:07:49.550 --> 00:07:50.959
much never seen again.

00:07:50.959 --> 00:07:53.089
Mary Jane is not my lover

00:07:53.089 --> 00:07:58.939
At some point during the 1990s, pop star Michael
Jackson made moves to try and purchase Marvel

00:07:58.939 --> 00:08:03.279
Comics, only because he wanted to play a heroic
character when it came time to make some Marvel

00:08:03.279 --> 00:08:04.279
movies.

00:08:04.279 --> 00:08:09.439
It was revealed in 2011 that Jackson petitioned
X-Men's producers for the role of Professor

00:08:09.439 --> 00:08:14.749
X, for reasons that are now a complete mystery
to all humanity, but Stan Lee's recollection

00:08:14.749 --> 00:08:15.749
was different.

00:08:15.749 --> 00:08:20.779
In their personal conversations, Jackson revealed
to Lee that he really wanted to play Spider-Man,

00:08:20.779 --> 00:08:25.080
though Tobey Maguire eventually got the role,
probably saving the Marvel movie universe

00:08:25.080 --> 00:08:26.689
as we know it.

00:08:26.689 --> 00:08:27.830
Trailer trashed

00:08:27.830 --> 00:08:32.380
Speaking of the original 2002 Spider-Man,
the film's original theatrical trailer had

00:08:32.380 --> 00:08:37.229
to be completely pulled and scrapped after
the events of 9/11, due to the fact that the

00:08:37.229 --> 00:08:41.870
entire thing hinged upon a helicopter being
caught on a web between the Twin Towers.

00:08:41.870 --> 00:08:45.990
Since the terrorist attacks happened after
much of filming was completed, but before

00:08:45.990 --> 00:08:50.610
the film's theatrical release, the Towers
were cut from a handful of scenes.

00:08:50.610 --> 00:08:55.370
The replacement trailer featured Spider-Man
perching on an American flag instead.

00:08:55.370 --> 00:08:56.430
Spandex sadness

00:08:56.430 --> 00:09:01.589
Spider-Man's many portrayers have all had
very different reactions to the tights.

00:09:01.589 --> 00:09:06.560
Civil War's Spider-Man, Tom Holland, had costumes
that were designed for his stunt double, so

00:09:06.560 --> 00:09:10.980
when he put them on, they were a bit too baggy,
bumming out Holland during his first time

00:09:10.980 --> 00:09:13.540
in the suit, according to Comic Book Movie.

00:09:13.540 --> 00:09:18.290
Tobey Maguire was pretty uncomfortable until
the costume designers put in a bathroom portal.

00:09:18.290 --> 00:09:22.940
And despite bathroom breaks being a known
problem for Spider-actors, the costume department

00:09:22.940 --> 00:09:28.029
again forgot to include a vital flap for Andrew
Garfield, who only had the addition put in

00:09:28.029 --> 00:09:30.230
during his second outing as the character.

00:09:30.230 --> 00:09:34.899
Still, Garfield admitted during a radio interview
that was so taken by the fact that he got

00:09:34.899 --> 00:09:39.120
to play the iconic hero that he cried when
he first put on the suit.

00:09:39.120 --> 00:09:41.740
And we thought Tobey was the Spidey who cried.

00:09:41.740 --> 00:09:42.910
Turn off the dark

00:09:42.910 --> 00:09:46.459
Spider-Man's films aren't the only times he's
been made into a live-action hero.

00:09:46.459 --> 00:09:53.329
In 2010, director Julie Taymor brought Spidey
to Broadway in a play so fraught with problems

00:09:53.329 --> 00:09:57.990
that it quickly became an unintentional comedy
of errors, even with music by the members

00:09:57.990 --> 00:09:58.990
of U2.

00:09:58.990 --> 00:10:04.080
By opening day, the production had already
cost $65 million, and not even Disney, who

00:10:04.080 --> 00:10:07.529
had recently purchased Marvel, had any interest
in helping out.

00:10:07.529 --> 00:10:11.830
A disturbing number of actors and extras were
injured during production due to the show's

00:10:11.830 --> 00:10:17.240
complex stunts, and at one point, Green Goblin
became stuck on a wire over the audience,

00:10:17.240 --> 00:10:20.709
hanging there for a few minutes before the
show could begin again.

00:10:20.709 --> 00:10:23.470
Not even the Clone Saga was this embarrassing.

00:10:23.470 --> 00:10:24.600
Spider sentai

00:10:24.600 --> 00:10:29.240
Broadway wasn't the only place to suffer from
a misguided live-action Spider-Man.

00:10:29.240 --> 00:10:35.360
In 1978, Japanese production company Toei
took note of Spidey's tight costume and likened

00:10:35.360 --> 00:10:39.069
him to their own skin-tight Tokusatsu heroes,
like Kamen Rider.

00:10:39.069 --> 00:10:43.800
Japan's Spider-Man was given an origin not
unlike DC's Green Lantern, when he's given

00:10:43.800 --> 00:10:48.990
a fancy device and powers by an alien from
the planet Spider, who he finds in a crashed

00:10:48.990 --> 00:10:50.769
UFO called "Marveller."

00:10:50.769 --> 00:10:56.610
In true Japan form, Spidey also gets a transforming
robot, which he uses to fight against Professor

00:10:56.610 --> 00:10:57.610
Monster.

00:10:57.610 --> 00:11:02.190
The show ran for 41 episodes, all with Marvel's
approval, and with amazing episode titles

00:11:02.190 --> 00:11:07.579
like "Becoming Splendid: To the Murderous
Machine of Transformation," and "The Onion

00:11:07.579 --> 00:11:09.779
Silver Mask and the Boys' Detective Group."

00:11:09.779 --> 00:11:11.860
Spider-Manimation

00:11:11.860 --> 00:11:17.889
In 1992, Marvel and Saban had a huge hit with
their X-Men animated series, so Marvel decided

00:11:17.889 --> 00:11:22.040
to build on this success by debuting Spider-Man
in 1994.

00:11:22.040 --> 00:11:27.790
While Spidey's series was also an action-figure-spawning
phenomenon, it was subject to completely absurd

00:11:27.790 --> 00:11:28.790
censorship.

00:11:28.790 --> 00:11:33.090
While it's reasonable to request that a kids'
cartoon omit animated blood, it made less

00:11:33.090 --> 00:11:38.399
sense to forbid even the mention of blood—even
in an episode where Morbius, the Living Vampire

00:11:38.399 --> 00:11:41.330
appears, and can no longer be called a vampire.

00:11:41.330 --> 00:11:46.290
Other rules included no breaking glass, no
punching—except in limited circumstances—and

00:11:46.290 --> 00:11:47.959
no use of the word "sinister."

00:11:47.959 --> 00:11:52.959
This last one is especially odd, as Spidey's
main villain collective is known as the Sinister

00:11:52.959 --> 00:11:57.160
Six, and Mr. Sinister was over on X-Men being
evil on the regular.

00:11:57.160 --> 00:12:02.180
Sorry, kids, basic English vocabulary is too
rough for your little pudding brains and bloodless

00:12:02.180 --> 00:12:03.180
bodies.

00:12:03.180 --> 00:12:04.189
Say no to bugs

00:12:04.189 --> 00:12:08.519
Because Spidey is pretty much an everyday,
average guy, he's been used countless times

00:12:08.519 --> 00:12:14.310
as a tool for educational purposes, from direct-to-VHS
shorts about child abuse to full comic arcs

00:12:14.310 --> 00:12:15.490
about the dangers of drugs.

00:12:15.490 --> 00:12:21.360
When the U.S. Government approached Stan Lee
to write an anti-drug story in 1971, he was

00:12:21.360 --> 00:12:24.129
all for it, as long as it was a good story.

00:12:24.129 --> 00:12:28.730
Strangely enough, this was during a time when
the Comics Code Authority also expressly forbade

00:12:28.730 --> 00:12:31.009
any mention of drugs whatsoever.

00:12:31.009 --> 00:12:35.390
After the story was shot down by the Comics
Code, Lee made the difficult decision to publish

00:12:35.390 --> 00:12:36.390
it anyway.

00:12:36.390 --> 00:12:41.060
It was so well-received that the CCA changed
its own code, opening up comics to a much

00:12:41.060 --> 00:12:43.699
wider range of educational stories.

00:12:43.699 --> 00:12:45.279
Oh baby

00:12:45.279 --> 00:12:50.089
Spidey's strangest foray into educational
materials was definitely "The Pull of the

00:12:50.089 --> 00:12:54.730
Prodigy," a 1976 story written by Ann Robinson
for Planned Parenthood.

00:12:54.730 --> 00:12:59.850
In an amazingly surreal plot, the alien villain
called Prodigy (no relation to Spider-Man's

00:12:59.850 --> 00:13:04.980
previously mentioned alter-alter-ego) uses
the powers of alien persuasion to try to convince

00:13:04.980 --> 00:13:09.829
teenagers to make babies...so that he'll have
tons of minions to do his bidding.

00:13:09.829 --> 00:13:14.260
Part of the Prodigy's propaganda was to straight
up say that diseases weren't possible and

00:13:14.260 --> 00:13:18.009
getting down in the backseat of a Chevy didn't
really cause pregnancies.

00:13:18.009 --> 00:13:22.410
Of course, Spidey laments how tough his life
is even without a rugrat, and defeats the

00:13:22.410 --> 00:13:23.999
Prodigy before it's too late.

00:13:23.999 --> 00:13:29.260
If anyone was poised to lecture kids about
celibacy, it was definitely nerdy Peter Parker.

00:13:29.260 --> 00:13:30.509
Thanks for watching!

00:13:30.509 --> 00:13:34.350
Subscribe to our YouTube channel to watch
more videos like the one you just saw!

00:13:34.350 --> 00:13:37.930
And leave us a comment to let you know what
Spidey fact surprised you the most...

